WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.615
The workbook's first
and fourth chapters make

2
00:00:02.615 --> 00:00:05.450
the front door testable
before anything is posted.

3
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:08.139
The contract lives in ServiceNow's script,

4
00:00:08.139 --> 00:00:11.288
so a Python mirror applies
the same rules offline.

5
00:00:11.288 --> 00:00:13.925
A test fails if the two ever drift apart.

6
00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:18.565
On five sample payloads, one gets a
default priority and a one-hour window,

7
00:00:18.565 --> 00:00:23.050
two are rejected with the script's own
error text, and one gets a warning.

8
00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.237
Its intended state names
nothing a re-read could check,

9
00:00:26.237 --> 00:00:28.362
so the record would close on nothing.

10
00:00:28.362 --> 00:00:33.051
Then the fourth chapter resolves each
SailPoint workflow against a saved trigger.

11
00:00:33.051 --> 00:00:34.687
That turned up two fixes.

12
00:00:34.687 --> 00:00:39.002
A change to a multi-valued attribute,
like an added proxy address,

13
00:00:39.002 --> 00:00:41.688
used to produce an empty intended state.

14
00:00:41.688 --> 00:00:46.738
And two attributes drifting on the same
user used to collapse into one record.

15
00:00:46.738 --> 00:00:51.620
Whether SailPoint resolves the body the
same way is still on the verify list.
